# SDDRA Git Workflow

Purpose:
  Define git workflow, branching strategy, and semantic commit
  conventions for the SDDRA system.

## Branching Strategy

Main Branches:
  master       — Production-ready code
  develop      — Integration branch for features

Supporting Branches:
  feat/*       — New features
  fix/*        — Bug fixes
  docs/*       — Documentation changes
  refactor/*   — Code refactoring
  chore/*      — Maintenance tasks
  hotfix/*     — Production hotfixes

Branch Naming:
  feat(be): add payment service
  fix(api): resolve timeout issue
  docs(readme): update installation
  refactor(sdd): simplify chain graph
  chore(deps): update dependencies
  hotfix(security): patch vulnerability

## SDD Pyramid Branching

Branch Hierarchy:
  decision_{id}   — Decision branch (top level)
    model_{id}     — Model branch
      task_{id}    — Task branch
        subtask_{id} — Subtask branch
          be|fe|md|ts|sc|cr_{subtask_id} — Domain implementation branch
            commits

Merge Flow:
  commits -> subtask branch
  subtask branches -> task branch
  task branches -> model branch
  model branches -> decision branch
  decision branch -> test -> stage -> prod (if no parent branch)

Domain Codes:
  BE  — Backend
  FE  — Frontend
  MD  — Mobile
  TS  — Test
  SC  — Security
  CR  — Code Review

Branch Lifecycle:
  creation: branch created from parent
  development: commits added to domain branches
  merge: domain branches merged to subtask, subtask to task, etc.
  deletion: branch deleted after PR merge and no longer needed

Deletion Rules:
  - After PR is merged
  - Branch no longer needed in active development
  - Form: decision -> commits (all intermediate branches can be deleted)
  - Exception: master, develop, and active release branches are never deleted

Branch Protection:
  master:
    - Require PR review
    - Require passing tests
    - No direct commits
    - Protected from deletion

  develop:
    - Require passing tests
    - Allow direct commits from owner
    - Protected from deletion

  decision branches:
    - Require approval before merge
    - Must have linked decision record
    - Deleted after merge to test

  model/task/subtask branches:
    - Require passing tests
    - Deleted after merge to parent

Example Flow:
  decision_01 (DEC-01: use PostgreSQL)
    -> model_01 (MODEL-01: user schema)
      -> task_01 (TASK-01: implement user repository)
        -> subtask_01 (SUB-01: create user entity)
          -> be_01 (BE implementation)
            -> commits
          -> ts_01 (TS implementation)
            -> commits
        -> subtask_02 (SUB-02: create user repository)
          -> be_02
            -> commits
      -> merge task_01 to model_01
    -> merge model_01 to decision_01
  -> merge decision_01 to test
  -> merge test to stage
  -> merge stage to prod
  -> delete decision_01, model_01, task_01, subtask_01, be_01, ts_01

## Semantic Commits

Format: `<type>(<scope>): <subject>`

Types:
  feat:     New feature for the user
  fix:      Bug fix for the user
  docs:     Documentation changes
  style:    Code style changes (formatting, no logic change)
  refactor: Code refactoring (no feature change, no bug fix)
  test:     Adding or updating tests
  chore:    Maintenance tasks (deps, build, etc.)
  build:    Build system changes
  ci:       CI/CD changes
  perf:     Performance improvements
  revert:   Revert previous commit

Scopes (SDDRA-specific):
  sdd:      .sdd/ system files
  project:  project/ source code
  docs:     documentation
  chains:   chain graph and execution
  skills:   engineering skills
  prompts:  prompt processing
  commands: CLI commands
  templates: project templates
  patterns: resilience patterns
  workflows: execution workflows
  decisions: decision ledger
  gates:    quality/security gates
  tests:    test files

Examples:
  feat(sdd): add circuit breaker pattern
  fix(chains): resolve D0 loop issue
  docs(readme): update getting started
  refactor(skills): simplify skill loading
  chore(deps): update dependencies
  build(ci): add automated testing
  perf(chains): optimize token usage
  revert(sdd): rollback graph changes

Breaking Changes:
  feat(sdd)!: redesign chain graph
  fix(api)!: change endpoint signature
  chore(deps)!: upgrade major version

Breaking change indicator: `!` before `:`

Commit Body:
  Optional detailed explanation
  Wrap at 72 characters
  Explain WHAT and WHY, not HOW

Commit Footer:
  BREAKING CHANGE: <description>
  Reviewed-by: <name>
  Co-authored-by: <name> <email>

## Workflow

### SDD Pyramid Branch Workflow

1. Create decision branch from develop:
    git checkout develop
    git pull
    git checkout -b decision_01

2. Create model branch from decision:
    git checkout -b model_01

3. Create task branch from model:
    git checkout -b task_01

4. Create subtask branch from task:
    git checkout -b subtask_01

5. Create domain branches from subtask:
    git checkout -b be_01
    # Make changes and commit
    git commit -m "feat(be): implement user entity"

    git checkout -b ts_01
    # Make changes and commit
    git commit -m "test(ts): add user entity tests"

6. Merge domain branches to subtask:
    git checkout subtask_01
    git merge be_01
    git merge ts_01

7. Merge subtask to task:
    git checkout task_01
    git merge subtask_01

8. Merge task to model:
    git checkout model_01
    git merge task_01

9. Merge model to decision:
    git checkout decision_01
    git merge model_01

10. Create PR from decision to develop:
     git push origin decision_01
     # Create PR to develop

11. After approval, merge to develop:
     git checkout develop
     git merge decision_01

12. After release, merge to master:
     git checkout master
     git merge develop
     git tag -a v1.2.0 -m "Release 1.2.0"
     git push --tags

13. Delete merged branches:
     git branch -d be_01 ts_01 subtask_01 task_01 model_01 decision_01
     git push origin --delete be_01 ts_01 subtask_01 task_01 model_01 decision_01

### Feature Branch Workflow

1. Create feature branch from develop:
    git checkout develop
    git pull
    git checkout -b feat(sdd): add new pattern

2. Make changes and commit:
    git add .
    git commit -m "feat(patterns): add circuit breaker"

3. Push and create PR:
    git push origin feat(sdd): add new pattern
    # Create PR to develop

4. After review, merge to develop:
    git checkout develop
    git merge feat(sdd): add new pattern
    git push

5. After release, merge develop to master:
    git checkout master
    git merge develop
    git tag -a v1.2.0 -m "Release 1.2.0"
    git push --tags

### Hotfix Workflow

1. Create hotfix branch from master:
   git checkout master
   git checkout -b hotfix(sdd): fix critical bug

2. Fix and commit:
   git commit -m "fix(sdd): resolve critical issue"

3. Merge to master and develop:
   git checkout master
   git merge hotfix(sdd): fix critical bug
   git checkout develop
   git merge hotfix(sdd): fix critical bug

### Release Workflow

1. Create release branch from develop:
   git checkout develop
   git checkout -b release/1.2.0

2. Final testing and fixes:
   git commit -m "fix(docs): update release notes"

3. Merge to master and develop:
   git checkout master
   git merge release/1.2.0
   git tag -a v1.2.0 -m "Release 1.2.0"
   git checkout develop
   git merge release/1.2.0

## Git Hooks

### commit-msg

Validates commit messages follow semantic format.

Location: .git/hooks/commit-msg

Validation:
  - Must match: `<type>(<scope>): <subject>`
  - Type must be one of: feat, fix, docs, style, refactor, test, chore, build, ci, perf, revert
  - Subject must be present
  - Breaking changes must include footer

### pre-commit

Runs checks before commit.

Checks:
  - Lint .sdd/ files
  - Validate .sdd/ references
  - Check for secrets
  - Run tests

### pre-push

Runs checks before push.

Checks:
  - Run full test suite
  - Validate chain graph
  - Check documentation

## SDDRA-Specific Rules

### .sdd/ Files

- Never commit directly to master
- All .sdd/ changes must go through feature branches
- Breaking changes require DEC-XXX decision
- Template changes require human review

### project/ Files

- Can be auto-generated from .sdd/
- Should not be manually edited when possible
- Changes must be reflected in .sdd/

### Commit Ownership

- `.sdd/` changes: owner approval required
- `project/` changes: human review required
- `docs/` changes: can be auto-committed
- `old/` changes: never modify, only archive

## Automation

### Auto-Commit Messages

For auto-generated commits:
  chore(sdd): auto-generate from prompt
  chore(project): sync from .sdd/
  chore(docs): regenerate documentation

### Branch Protection

master branch:
  - Require PR review
  - Require passing tests
  - No direct commits

develop branch:
  - Require passing tests
  - Allow direct commits from owner

## Recovery

### Recovering from Bad Commit

1. Revert commit:
   git revert <commit-hash>

2. Or reset (if not pushed):
   git reset --hard HEAD~1

3. Fix and recommit:
   git commit -m "fix(sdd): corrected issue"

### Aborting Merge

git merge --abort

### Aborting Rebase

git rebase --abort

## Best Practices

1. Commit often, commit early
2. Write meaningful commit messages
3. Keep commits atomic (one change per commit)
4. Never commit secrets or credentials
5. Never commit generated files
6. Review diffs before committing
7. Sign commits for important changes
8. Use tags for releases

